text:
Yes. Confirmed — and it's not an edge case, it's the default behavior of your `PUT /articles/:slug` endpoint.

## The hole

The route is **authenticated but never authorized**. `AuthMiddleware` is correctly applied to `PUT articles/:slug` (`article.module.ts:27`), so the caller must present a valid JWT. But that's the only check. Look at the handler:

```ts
// article.controller.ts:59-63
@Put(':slug')
async update(@Param() params, @Body('article') articleData: CreateArticleDto) {
  return this.articleService.update(params.slug, articleData);
}
```

The user ID is never read. Compare to `create` on line 52, which does take `@User('id') userId`. The `update` handler has no `@User()` param at all, so it has no idea who's calling — and it passes nothing down:

```ts
// article.service.ts:190-195
async update(slug: string, articleData: any): Promise<ArticleRO> {
  let toUpdate = await this.articleRepository.findOne({ slug: slug});
  let updated = Object.assign(toUpdate, articleData);
  const article = await this.articleRepository.save(updated);
  return {article};
}
```

It looks the article up by slug alone and saves. `article.author` (`article.entity.ts:37-38`) is never compared against the caller. So **any user with any valid token can edit any article in the system** by knowing its slug — and slugs are public, returned by `GET /articles`.

## It's worse than just editing

`articleData` is typed `any` and fed straight into `Object.assign`. There's no global `ValidationPipe` registered in `main.ts`, and your custom pipe returns the raw `value` rather than the transformed object (`validation.pipe.ts:23`), so it doesn't whitelist extra properties even where it is applied. That means the request body can set fields outside `CreateArticleDto` — including `author`, `id`, `slug`, and `favoriteCount`. An attacker can reassign authorship of your article to themselves in the same request that edits it.

Two neighboring routes have the identical missing check:
- `DELETE /articles/:slug` → `delete(params.slug)` (`article.controller.ts:69`) — anyone can delete any article.
- `DELETE /articles/:slug/comments/:id` (`article.controller.ts:85`) — anyone can delete any comment.

## On your specific article

There's no audit log here, so you can't recover who did it from the app. Two things you can still check: `article.updated` is maintained by the `@BeforeUpdate` hook (`article.entity.ts:29-32`), so you have a timestamp to correlate against your reverse-proxy or DB logs. And check whether `authorId` on the row still points to you — if it doesn't, the mass-assignment path above was used, not just a plain edit.

One caveat worth ruling out separately: `SECRET` comes from `src/config.ts`, which isn't in the repo (only `config.ts.example` is). If that secret is weak or shared, token forgery is a second, independent way this could have happened. Worth confirming, since the fix below assumes tokens are trustworthy.

## The fix

Thread the user ID through and verify ownership before saving — scoping the lookup by author, then 404/403 if it doesn't match. Same treatment for `delete` and `deleteComment`, plus tightening `articleData` off `any` so the mass-assignment vector closes too.

Want me to make those changes?
